<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/expert/ai-alignment-and-interpretability">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    AI alignment is <strong>the problem of making AI systems do what people actually intend</strong>
    — not just what their training reward says. As systems grow more capable, the gap between
    specified goals and intended goals becomes the main safety risk. Interpretability (opening
    the box) and alignment research (steering what's inside) are the responses — part
    engineering, part philosophy, entirely necessary.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the alignment problem, its best current tools, and the open agenda" widget="equationStep" config={alignCfg} />

  <h2>The honest state of the field</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Current systems fail charmingly, not dangerously:</strong> hallucination, sycophancy (agreeing with users), goal misgeneralisation. Failure modes scale with capability — the research question is whether fixes scale too.</li>
    <li><strong>Interpretability wins are real:</strong> researchers found and edited specific features (a "golden gate bridge" feature in a production LLM), traced deception-like circuits in game-playing models, and can sometimes steer refusal behaviour. Early days, real progress.</li>
    <li><strong>Disagreement is part of the field:</strong> timelines, severity and the right framework (technical vs governance-first) are actively contested among serious researchers. This lesson presents the shared toolkit, not a prophecy.</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters beyond AI labs:</strong> every deployed system — medical, legal, infrastructural — is alignment-relevant today. The near-term work (evaluation, red-teaming, interpretability) is the same discipline the evaluation lesson described, applied with more paranoia.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Amodei et al., "Concrete Problems in AI Safety" (2016); Ouyang et al., InstructGPT/RLHF (2022); Anthropic interpretability research (2023–24); Russell, Human Compatible (2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
